Appearance: The Money Plant features heart-shaped leaves that are typically a vibrant green color, although some varieties may have variegated leaves with white or yellow streaks. The leaves grow on long, trailing stems, creating a cascading effect when grown in hanging baskets or containers.

Cultivation: Money Plants are adaptable and can thrive in a variety of indoor environments. They prefer bright, indirect light but can also tolerate lower light conditions. Allow the soil to dry out between waterings, as overwatering can lead to root rot.

Air Purification: Money Plants are known for their ability to purify indoor air by removing toxins such as formaldehyde, benzene, and xylene. This makes them beneficial additions to homes and offices, helping to improve air quality and create a healthier living environment.

Propagation: Money Plants are easy to propagate from stem cuttings. Simply cut a section of stem with a few nodes and place it in water or moist soil until roots develop. Once rooted, the cutting can be potted up into its own container.

Maintenance: Money Plants are relatively low-maintenance and require minimal care once established. Prune the plant occasionally to remove dead or yellowing leaves and encourage bushier growth. Fertilize sparingly during the growing season to promote healthy growth.', NULL, NULL, '2026-08-17T15:47:40.040Z', '2026-08-20T11:38:39.535Z', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Money Plant, also known as Pothos or Devil''s Ivy (Epipremnum aureum), is a popular and easy-to-care-for houseplant known for its lush foliage and air-puri', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'Money Plant');
